# Four directories in this task are Docker build contexts -- `environment/` and the
# three under `tests/` -- and this same list ships in all four, byte for byte.
# Nothing below is a file any of them needs: it is the debris that accumulates from
# running the suite locally, plus editor backups.
#
# The three stage Dockerfiles copy the whole of their context (`COPY . /tests/…`),
# so anything left lying about in one of those gets baked in. `environment/` is the
# asymmetric one -- it names each of the files it copies, so this list filters
# nothing there today. It ships there anyway, and identical rather than trimmed to
# what that context could plausibly collect: four copies of one file is a property
# `check-task.py` can check, and the alternative is a build context whose exclusions
# nothing compares against the other three.
#
# Every pattern carries a `**/` prefix, and that is not decoration. Docker matches a
# .dockerignore pattern against the whole context-relative path and does *not*
# re-apply it at each directory level the way .gitignore does, so a bare
# `__pycache__/` excludes a __pycache__ at the context root and nothing else -- not
# `lib/__pycache__`, which is the one that actually appears here, because every
# check in this suite imports `catalog` and `gen`. The pattern is deliberately not a
# list of the directories where bytecode has been seen: a list goes stale while
# `**/` keeps working.
#
# PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE in the Dockerfile does not cover this. That stops the
# *image* writing bytecode and has no effect on COPY bringing the author's bytecode
# in. The two failures are indistinguishable from inside the Dockerfile and differ
# only by the interpreter tag on the filename -- the author's is `cpython-310`, the
# image's would be `cpython-311`.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/*~
**/*.orig
**/*.rej
**/.DS_Store

# JavaScript and TypeScript debris, and this pair is why the list matters more here
# than in the tasks that came before it.
#
# `dist/` is the delivered surface of this repository: State A's build writes it,
# `build` grades it, and `structure` asks whether it is self-contained. An author
# who ran `npm run build` inside a stage directory to check something by hand would
# otherwise ship that output into a grading image -- and unlike a stray `obj/`, a
# stray `dist/` here would be *a working JavaScript implementation of jsonata*
# sitting in the image whose whole design is that no such thing is present. The
# final stage of `tests/behavioural/Dockerfile` sweeps for exactly these filenames
# and would fail the build, which is the intended outcome; this list is what stops
# it happening in the first place.
#
# `node_modules` for the same reason one step removed: the repository has no
# dependencies, so a `node_modules` in a context is either a hand-installed tsc or
# a hand-installed jsonata, and the second is the reference by another name.
**/dist
**/node_modules
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/npm-debug.log
**/.npm

# Work directories. `identity.py` and `freeze.py` both take a `--work` and both are
# run by hand during authoring; the trees they leave behind hold a built copy of
# State A. Excluded by name rather than trusted to live outside the context.
**/work
**/identity
